namespace routes.site;

import middleware.metrics;

/// Everything this service serves that is not a link.
///
/// The landing page, the crawler files, the OG image, the OpenAPI
/// document and the Swagger page are all **files**. `static "/" from
/// "public"` serves them, and under `jwc build` the tree is walked at
/// compile time and `include_bytes!`d into the binary (routing.md §10.6),
/// so the container needs no directory beside it.
///
/// Until this port the landing page was 360 lines of `+ "\n"` string
/// concatenation inside a `views.pages` function, because 0.9's HTML lived
/// in a `r"..."` literal and 1.0 has no multi-line string. Neither form
/// was the right one: it is a file, it never changes per request, and as a
/// file it gets an ETag, a 304 and a `Cache-Control` for free.
///
/// `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml` and `docs/index.html` were routes here
/// until jwc 0.9.942, for one reason: routing.md §10.2 put *every* route
/// ahead of a `static` mount, and `/{code: text}` is a route — so
/// `/robots.txt` reached the redirect handler, found no such link and
/// answered 404. §10.2 now ranks a mount ahead of a route that bound a
/// path parameter, which is what §4.3 said all along (a file under a
/// mount is all-literal; a `{slot}` has none), so they are files again.
///
/// What that costs: a mount takes no middleware (§10.1), so these four
/// paths are no longer counted by `MetricsTracker`. That is the trade —
/// crawler traffic drops out of `/ops/stats`, and in exchange the pages
/// are cached, revalidated and served without touching the database.
static "/" from "public" cache 3600;

/// The two URLs the 0.9 service published, kept working.
///
/// The documents live under `/assets/` — one place for everything
/// `jwc openapi` and the design step write — and a 301 is what tells a
/// social scraper with a cached card, or a client with a saved OpenAPI
/// URL, where they went. These stay routes because they are redirects,
/// not files.
routes "/" use MetricsTracker {
    route GET "og.svg" {
        return redirect(301, "/assets/og.svg");
    }

    route GET "openapi.json" {
        return redirect(301, "/assets/openapi.json");
    }
}
